## Advanced Iterator Techniques

### chain — Concatenate Iterators
```xiom
let a = [1, 2, 3].iter();
let b = [4, 5, 6].iter();
let combined = a.chain(b);
// Produces: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6
```

### zip — Pair Elements
```xiom
let names = ["Alice", "Bob"].iter();
let scores = [85, 92].iter();
let paired = names.zip(scores);
// Produces: ("Alice", 85), ("Bob", 92)
```

### enumerate — Add Index
```xiom
let items = ["a", "b", "c"].iter();
for (i, item) in items.enumerate() {
  io.println(i.to_str() + ": " + item);
}
// 0: a, 1: b, 2: c
```

### flat_map — Flatten Nested Iterators
```xiom
let words = ["hello", "world"].iter();
let chars = words.flat_map(|w| w.chars());
// Produces: 'h', 'e', 'l', 'l', 'o', 'w', 'o', 'r', 'l', 'd'
```

### peekable — Look Ahead
```xiom
let mut iter = [1, 2, 3].iter().peekable();
let next_val = iter.peek();  // Some(1) — doesn't advance
let actual = iter.next();     // Some(1) — now advances
```

### Pattern: Sliding Window
```xiom
fn window_sum(data: &Vec[Int], size: Int) -> Vec[Int] {
  return data.windows(size).map(|w| w.sum()).collect();
}
```

### Pattern: Group By
```xiom
fn group_by_first_char(words: &Vec[Str]) -> Map[Char, Vec[Str]] {
  var groups = Map[Char, Vec[Str]]();
  for word in words {
    let first = word[0];
    groups.entry(first).or_default().push(word);
  }
  return groups;
}
```

